# Stationer & Dry Goods, Business District.
# Mr. Finch: local merchant, sells ledgers, twine, and wax; observer of town habits.
NPC: stationer
LOCATION: business_district
SCHEDULE: morning=stationer, midday=stationer, evening=stationer, night=closed

TOPIC: default
  GATE: NOT topic_done(stationer, default) AND coat = plain
  TAG: stationer_plain
  VOICE: trombone_neutral_medium_v1
  MR. FINCH: "Two-cent ruled slates on the counter, linen correspondence paper in the drawers."
  [He wraps a cake of red sealing wax in brown paper and ties it with a short length of hemp twine.]
  VOICE: trombone_neutral_medium_v1
  MR. FINCH: "You have ink on your knuckles, friend. Same as any man who spends his evenings arguing with ledgers."

TOPIC: default
  GATE: NOT topic_done(stationer, default)
  TAG: stationer_badge
  VOICE: trombone_bureaucratic_medium_v1
  MR. FINCH: "Officer Corwin. If this is the quarterly precinct supply voucher, Odell hasn't endorsed it."
  WALTER CORWIN: "I'm not here for blotters, Mr. Finch."
  VOICE: trombone_bureaucratic_medium_v1
  MR. FINCH: "Then I hope you're here as a paying customer.\nA shop runs on sales, Officer, not on town curiosity."

TOPIC: default
  GATE: visit_count(stationer) >= 2 AND NOT (topic_done(stationer, the_corkboard_twine) AND topic_done(stationer, fenn_orders) AND topic_done(stationer, naomi_supplies))
  TAG: stationer_repeat_ledger
  WEIGHT: 3
  VOICE: trombone_neutral_short_v1
  MR. FINCH: "Ink, blotters, or more twine for your case, Walter?"

TOPIC: default
  GATE: visit_count(stationer) >= 2 AND NOT (topic_done(stationer, the_corkboard_twine) AND topic_done(stationer, fenn_orders) AND topic_done(stationer, naomi_supplies))
  TAG: stationer_repeat_paper
  WEIGHT: 2
  [He trims the edge of a stack of foolscap with a heavy cast-iron guillotine shear.]
  VOICE: trombone_neutral_medium_v1
  MR. FINCH: "Step back from the shear. What else needs recording?"

TOPIC: default
  GATE: visit_count(stationer) >= 2 AND NOT (topic_done(stationer, the_corkboard_twine) AND topic_done(stationer, fenn_orders) AND topic_done(stationer, naomi_supplies))
  TAG: stationer_repeat_quill
  WEIGHT: 1
  VOICE: trombone_bureaucratic_short_v2
  MR. FINCH: "The ledgers don't change between morning and midday, Officer."

TOPIC: default
  GATE: topic_done(stationer, the_corkboard_twine) AND topic_done(stationer, fenn_orders) AND topic_done(stationer, naomi_supplies)
  TAG: stationer_exhausted_ink
  WEIGHT: 3
  VOICE: trombone_dismissive_short_v1
  MR. FINCH: "The stock is accounted for, Walter. If you don't need paper, let the trade go on."

TOPIC: default
  GATE: topic_done(stationer, the_corkboard_twine) AND topic_done(stationer, fenn_orders) AND topic_done(stationer, naomi_supplies)
  TAG: stationer_exhausted_quiet
  WEIGHT: 2
  [He dips a steel pen in black ink, blotting the nib with practiced ease.]
  VOICE: trombone_neutral_short_v2
  MR. FINCH: "Nothing more in the day-books. You've seen the whole of it."

TOPIC: the_corkboard_twine
  GATE: always
  TAG: stationer_twine
  LABEL: "Ask about heavy wrapping twine and map pins"
  TIME: 7
  WALTER CORWIN: "I need another spool of the tarred hemp twine. And brass-headed pins."
  VOICE: trombone_weary_medium_v2
  MR. FINCH: "Working late in your room above the cobbler again, are you?"
  [He sets a wooden bobbin of dark twine on the counter.]
  VOICE: trombone_bureaucratic_long_v1
  MR. FINCH: "Most men who buy that twine use it to hang dried cod in the shed.\nYou're the only man in this county who strings it between index cards."
  WALTER CORWIN: "It keeps the facts from drifting."
  VOICE: trombone_neutral_long_v1
  MR. FINCH: "Facts don't drift, Walter. Men do.\nWhen my ledger shows an inventory deficit, I don't string twine across the shelves. I look at who holds the key."
  NOTEBOOK: stationer_twine_1 | "Mr. Finch: 'Facts don't drift, Walter. Men do.' Observed that only Walter uses merchant twine to bind cards."

TOPIC: fenn_orders
  GATE: evidence(knife)
  TAG: stationer_parchment
  LABEL: "Ask about Dr. Fenn's stationery orders"
  TIME: 6
  WALTER CORWIN: "Dr. Aldous Fenn bought his calfskin notebooks here."
  VOICE: trombone_neutral_long_v1
  MR. FINCH: "Heavy imported vellum. Octavo size, marbled endpapers.\nHe insisted on iron-gall ink. Said modern aniline dyes fade inside thirty years, and he intended his observations to outlast the century."
  WALTER CORWIN: "What was he writing?"
  VOICE: trombone_bureaucratic_long_v1
  MR. FINCH: "He didn't share his notes with a tradesman, Officer.\nThough he did buy two dozen wax candles every autumn. The thick spermaceti ones that burn without smoke in closed cellars."
  NOTEBOOK: stationer_fenn_supplies | "Dr. Fenn purchased iron-gall ink and heavy smokeless spermaceti candles for closed-cellar work."
  EVIDENCE: cellar_candles

# Redundant-carrier backup: if local_historian's survey fork was missed or taken
# for annotations, this supplies the same EVIDENCE for the ropewalk foreman lead.
TOPIC: fenn_conduit_tracing
  GATE: evidence(cellar_candles) AND NOT evidence(estate_conduit_map)
  TAG: stationer_conduit_tracing
  LABEL: "Ask if Dr. Fenn copied architectural or drainage surveys"
  TIME: 6
  WALTER CORWIN: "When Dr. Fenn bought drafting materials, did he say what he was copying?"
  VOICE: trombone_bureaucratic_long_v1
  MR. FINCH: "He brought in a rolled plate from the historical collection — an 1822 survey of the kitchen bluff.\nWanted tracing vellum cut to match the subterranean drainage conduits running beneath the estate.\nSaid municipal works had forgotten where the old stone drains exited to the salt water."
  NOTEBOOK: stationer_fenn_conduits | "Mr. Finch recalls Dr. Fenn purchasing vellum to trace an 1822 survey of the kitchen bluff's subterranean drainage conduits running to salt water."
  EVIDENCE: estate_conduit_map

TOPIC: naomi_supplies
  GATE: evidence(naomi)
  TAG: stationer_naomi
  LABEL: "Ask if Naomi Freeman purchased writing materials"
  TIME: 7
  WALTER CORWIN: "Did Naomi Freeman come into the shop before she died?"
  VOICE: trombone_questioning_medium_v2
  MR. FINCH: "The colored woman staying at Mrs. Almy's? She came in on a Thursday."
  WALTER CORWIN: "What did she buy?"
  VOICE: trombone_neutral_long_v1
  MR. FINCH: "A one-cent graphite pencil. And two sheets of heavy draughting linen.\nShe asked if linen would rot if kept in oilskin near salt water. I told her linen outlasts paper three to one in the damp."
  [He pauses, resting both palms on the polished counter.]
  VOICE: trombone_neutral_long_v1
  MR. FINCH: "She wasn't writing a letter, Walter. She was copying a diagram.\nShe asked if I carried red transfer wax to take an impression of an engraved brass maker's plate.\nSaid she needed to lift the serial mark off an old boiler without damaging the patina."
  NOTEBOOK: stationer_naomi_linen | "Naomi Freeman bought drafting linen and asked for transfer wax to take an impression of an engraved brass plate on an old boiler without damaging patina."
  EVIDENCE: drafting_linen_diagram

# Redundant-carrier backup: if gazette_editor is missed, this supplies the same
# EVIDENCE so downstream gates (press_the_arithmetic, correction_terms) still open.
TOPIC: halleck_arithmetic
  GATE: evidence(eight) AND NOT evidence(press_suppression)
  TAG: stationer_halleck_arithmetic
  LABEL: "Ask if Finch has heard how the Gazette explains the six count"
  TIME: 7
  WALTER CORWIN: "Eight people died in that garden. The Gazette prints six."
  VOICE: trombone_neutral_medium_v2
  MR. FINCH: "Halleck settles his ink account here every first of the month. He said something of the sort to me directly."
  [He sets down the guillotine shear, unhurried.]
  VOICE: trombone_bureaucratic_long_v1
  MR. FINCH: "Six subscribers, he called it. Said a transient's obituary doesn't sell newsprint, and a paper that can't sell newsprint can't pay its ink account.\nI didn't ask him to justify it twice. A shopkeeper doesn't quarrel with a paying customer's arithmetic."
  NOTEBOOK: stationer_halleck_arithmetic_1 | "Mr. Finch reports Halleck settled his ink account with the same reasoning given Walter directly: six subscribers count, a transient's death does not sell newsprint."
  EVIDENCE: press_suppression

# Shared inquiry: four different completed refusals make the question travel.
TOPIC: crew_omission
  GATE: topic_done(local_historian, crew_omission_official)
  TAG: stationer_crew_omission
  LABEL: "Ask why the school reader never names the crew"
  TIME: 5
  WALTER CORWIN: "Eight men drowned in 1823. The school reader doesn't spare them so much as their names."
  VOICE: trombone_neutral_medium_v2
  MR. FINCH: "I sell the school reader as it comes from the printer."
  WALTER CORWIN: "No one asked why it names the benefactors and not the crew?"
  VOICE: trombone_neutral_medium_v1
  MR. FINCH: "No one asked me. If you want the wording changed, you need someone other than a stationer."
  NOTEBOOK: crew_omission_refusal | "Asked Mr. Finch why the crew's names are missing from the town's history. No explanation supplied; the question remains open."
